1 DEFINE

   Creates a logical name, or replaces one defined in the same table at
   the same access mode.

   Format

     DEFINE logical-name equivalence-string[,...]

   DEFINE/DEVICE defines a device instead (see DEFINE /DEVICE).

2 Parameters

 logical-name

   The name to define.  A trailing colon is kept as part of the name.

 equivalence-string[,...]

   What the logical name stands for.  More than one makes the name a
   search list, translated to each string in turn.  Unquoted text is
   made uppercase.

2 Qualifiers
/EXECUTIVE_MODE

   Defines the name at executive mode.

/GROUP

   Defines the name in the group table, LNM$GROUP.

/JOB

   Defines the name in the job table, LNM$JOB, shared by the console's
   process and every subprocess it creates.

/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   Reports when the name replaces one defined before in the same table
   at the same mode (the default, /LOG).

/PROCESS

   Defines the name in the process table, LNM$PROCESS (the default).

/SUPERVISOR_MODE

   Defines the name at supervisor mode (the default).

/SYSTEM

   Defines the name in the system table, LNM$SYSTEM.

/TABLE

      /TABLE=name

   Defines the name in the table named, or the table a logical name
   such as LNM$PROCESS translates to.

/TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES

      /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(keyword[,...])

   CONCEALED shows the logical name in place of the device it stands
   for, and TERMINAL stops iterative translation at this name.  The
   attributes apply to every equivalence string.

/USER_MODE

   Defines the name at user mode.  A user-mode name in the process
   table is deleted when the next image exits: one RUN runs, or a
   command VMS runs as an image, such as MACRO, LINK, or TYPE.

2 Examples

   1.$ DEFINE WORK DUA1:[WORK]

     Defines WORK in the process table, at supervisor mode.

   2.$ DEFINE/SYSTEM/TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=CONCEALED DISK$DATA DUA2:

     Defines a concealed device name for every process.

   3.$ DEFINE/USER SYS$OUTPUT LISTING.TXT

     Sends the next image's output to LISTING.TXT, until it exits.

2 /DEVICE

   Defines a device: a name in the device table, with the
   characteristics $GETDVI and F$GETDVI report for it.  STARTUP.COM
   defines the terminal TTA0: and the null device NLA0: this way; MOUNT
   makes a disk's device itself, so DEFINE/DEVICE is seldom needed.  A
   characteristic not given is 0.  Defining a name again makes a new
   device of that name, which is found in place of the old one.

   Format

     DEFINE/DEVICE device-name

3 Parameter

 device-name

   The device's name, such as TTA0 or NLA0, without the colon.

3 Qualifiers
/CLUSTER

      /CLUSTER=n

   The volume's cluster size, in blocks.

/CYLINDERS

      /CYLINDERS=n

   The number of cylinders.

/DEVBUFSIZE

      /DEVBUFSIZE=n

   The default buffer size, in bytes: a terminal's width, or a
   mailbox's largest message.

/DEVCHAR

      /DEVCHAR=n

   The device characteristics longword, DVI$_DEVCHAR: DEV$M_ bits, such
   as record-oriented, terminal, or file-oriented.

/DEVCHAR2

      /DEVCHAR2=n

   The second characteristics longword, DVI$_DEVCHAR2.

/DEVCLASS

      /DEVCLASS=keyword

   The device class: DISK, TAPE, SCOM, CARD, TERM, LP, WORKSTATION,
   REALTIME, DECVOICE, AUDIO, VIDEO, BUS, MAILBOX, RECMSL_STORAGE, or
   MISC.

/DEVDEPEND

      /DEVDEPEND=n

   The device-dependent longword, DVI$_DEVDEPEND.

/DEVDEPEND2

      /DEVDEPEND2=n

   The second device-dependent longword, DVI$_DEVDEPEND2.

/DEVTYPE

      /DEVTYPE=keyword

   The device type: a disk's model (RD54, RA92, RX50, ...), VT100 for a
   terminal, or NULL for the null device.

/FREEBLOCKS

      /FREEBLOCKS=n

   The free blocks reported when no volume is mounted.

/LOCKID

      /LOCKID=n

   The device's lock ID, DVI$_LOCKID.

/MAXBLOCK

      /MAXBLOCK=n

   The number of blocks the device holds.

/MAXFILES

      /MAXFILES=n

   The largest number of files its volume may hold.

/MEDIANAME

      /MEDIANAME=string

   The media name, DVI$_MEDIA_NAME.

/MEDIATYPE

      /MEDIATYPE=string

   The media type, DVI$_MEDIA_TYPE.

/OWNUIC

      /OWNUIC=n

   The owner's UIC, as a longword (65540 is [1,4]).

/RECSIZE

      /RECSIZE=n

   The record size, DVI$_RECSIZ.

/ROOTDEVNAME

      /ROOTDEVNAME=string

   The root device's name, DVI$_ROOTDEVNAM.

/SECTORS

      /SECTORS=n

   The number of sectors per track.

/SERIAL

      /SERIAL=n

   The serial number, DVI$_SERIALNUM.

/VOLNAME

      /VOLNAME=string

   The volume label reported when no volume is mounted.

3 Examples

   1.$ DEFINE/DEVICE NLA0/DEVCLASS=MAILBOX/DEVTYPE=NULL/DEVBUFSIZE=512

     Defines a null device, as STARTUP.COM does (with more of its
     characteristics).
